**Solution.**

Since $a,b>0$, the function $f(x)=(x+a)(x+b)=x^2+(a+b)x+ab$ is strictly increasing on $[0,\infty)$ (its derivative $2x+(a+b)>0$). Hence for $x_i,x_j\ge 0$,
$$
\min\{f(x_i),f(x_j)\}=f(\min\{x_i,x_j\}).
$$

The expression $F=\sum_{i<j}\min\{f(x_i),f(x_j)\}$ is symmetric in the $x_i$, so we may relabel and assume
$$
x_1\ge x_2\ge\cdots\ge x_n\ge 0,\qquad \sum_{i=1}^n x_i=1.
$$
Then for $i<j$ we have $\min\{x_i,x_j\}=x_j$, and $x_j$ is the smaller element in exactly the $j-1$ pairs $(1,j),(2,j),\dots,(j-1,j)$. Therefore
$$
F=\sum_{j=1}^{n}(j-1)\,f(x_j). \tag{1}
$$

Since $f$ is convex ($f''=2>0$) and the weights $j-1\ge 0$, the right-hand side of (1) is a **convex** function of $(x_1,\dots,x_n)$. We are maximizing a convex function over the compact convex polytope
$$
\mathcal P=\{x\in\mathbb R^n:\ x_1\ge x_2\ge\cdots\ge x_n\ge 0,\ \textstyle\sum x_i=1\}.
$$
A convex function on a compact convex polytope attains its maximum at an extreme point. The extreme points (vertices) of $\mathcal P$ are well known:
$$
v^{(k)}=\Bigl(\underbrace{\tfrac1k,\dots,\tfrac1k}_{k},\,0,\dots,0\Bigr),\qquad k=1,2,\dots,n.
$$
(Each vertex is obtained by making $n-1$ of the defining inequalities tight; the only possibilities are $x_1=\cdots=x_k=1/k$, $x_{k+1}=\cdots=x_n=0$.)

So it suffices to evaluate $F$ at each $v^{(k)}$. At $v^{(k)}$,
$$
F(v^{(k)})=\sum_{j=1}^{k}(j-1)f(1/k)+\sum_{j=k+1}^{n}(j-1)f(0).
$$
Using $f(1/k)=\frac1{k^2}+\frac{a+b}{k}+ab$, $f(0)=ab$, $\sum_{j=1}^{k}(j-1)=\frac{k(k-1)}2$, and $\sum_{j=k+1}^{n}(j-1)=\frac{n(n-1)}2-\frac{k(k-1)}2$, we get
$$
F(v^{(k)})=\frac{k(k-1)}2\Bigl(\frac1{k^2}+\frac{a+b}{k}\Bigr)+\frac{n(n-1)}2\,ab
=\underbrace{\frac{k-1}{2k}+\frac{(k-1)(a+b)}2}_{g(k)}+\frac{n(n-1)}2\,ab.
$$
The last term is constant, so we maximize $g(k)$ over $k=1,\dots,n$. Compute the forward difference:
$$
g(k+1)-g(k)=\Bigl[\frac{k}{2(k+1)}-\frac{k-1}{2k}\Bigr]+\frac{a+b}{2}
=\frac{1}{2k(k+1)}+\frac{a+b}{2}>0,
$$
since $a+b>0$. Thus $g(k)$ is **strictly increasing**, and the maximum occurs at $k=n$, i.e. at the uniform point $x_1=\cdots=x_n=\frac1n$.

Substituting $k=n$:
$$
F_{\max}=\frac{n-1}{2n}+\frac{(n-1)(a+b)}2+\frac{n(n-1)}2\,ab
=\frac{n-1}{2n}\Bigl(1+n(a+b)+n^2ab\Bigr)
=\frac{n-1}{2n}(1+na)(1+nb).
$$

**Verification.** At $x_i=\frac1n$ for all $i$, every $f(x_i)=f(1/n)=(\frac1n+a)(\frac1n+b)$, and there are $\binom n2=\frac{n(n-1)}2$ pairs, so $F=\frac{n(n-1)}2(\frac1n+a)(\frac1n+b)=\frac{n-1}{2n}(1+na)(1+nb)$, matching the formula. ✓

**Maximum value:**
$$
\boxed{\,F_{\max}=\dfrac{n-1}{2n}\,(1+na)(1+nb)\,}
$$
attained when $x_1=x_2=\cdots=x_n=\dfrac1n$.

